#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328751 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "624215ff-efdc-5b12-8d61-9d66371672d9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Valid Region to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86359748-2710-5442-b39a-0216502c173c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328753} {:answer/id #uuid "32d62bc0-70da-512e-bcaa-d114ee699ec2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328754} {:answer/id #uuid "f18cd051-448b-55a5-bf30-cbbf39cd2421" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328755} {:answer/id #uuid "b184a5e0-cbae-5f9b-a9af-a07a59b98e42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328756} {:answer/id #uuid "9b41d158-236b-5fab-9b72-02b0cce1a55d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328757}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b41d158-236b-5fab-9b72-02b0cce1a55d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328757} :answer-field/id #uuid "f03bdbab-6e0f-52e4-afcd-d98dc78cb2a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328752}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ea5c7d0f-2337-570f-834e-ac4ea69d4a93" :question/math-academy-id "q-86861" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050897/assets/534f6ef0206bd4281320968c8a60b88f7f237e48c31e3d10c1080ddb69b9f56f.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{3}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\n- $R=[-1,2]\\times [0,1]$\n- $R=[-1,2]\\times [-2,2]$\n- $R=[-2,2]\\times [0,1]$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{3}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangles I and III do not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Rectangle II covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}]}
